`@entry.moth` is the executable entry root. Its presence makes the directory entry-eligible. Top-level runtime work in that file becomes a dormant compiler-owned entry, which entry assembly activates exactly once for a selected page. `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` are API-only facades with no start function and no fragments, and a dependency never activates its entry.

### Entry ownership

Every selected module compiles into an immutable module artefact. Only a module with `@entry.moth` owns dormant root runtime, direct page fragments and an implicit `start`. An API facade may declare functions and constants but rejects top-level runtime statements, direct fragments and builder slots.

The entry still has its built-in `Error!` contract. Checked numeric failure propagates to that contract, and compatible top-level postfix `!` is legal. Custom typed errors still need explicit recovery. A generated terminal handler closes the entry boundary, so an unhandled built-in failure does not become an implicit panic. See @../progress (Progress) for compiler support.

### Entry eligibility and activation

Eligibility follows file presence. An empty `@entry.moth`, a static-only entry and a metadata-only entry all count. No activity heuristic selects a page.

Entry assembly activates the selected module-owned entry exactly once. Loading a module, binding its API or importing a package exposes declarations without activating root work. A project-local `@entry.moth` is the only project entry source. Dependency entries and separately compiled source-package entries never activate.

Only a direct top-level const `$in` on the entry reaches the builder. A nested or aliased insert remains ordinary template composition, and runtime top-level templates still contribute only to the default body lane. Runtime named builder slots are not supported.

### Comment and documentation templates

Semantically classified `$doc`, `$note` and `$todo` templates are documentation or comment processing. They produce no page fragment. This follows the template's semantic category, not whether its rendered output happens to be empty. The entry remains eligible because the file exists.

Assigned or returned template values are ordinary values, not direct output. Their containing runtime code follows the normal entry placement rules.

### Entry-activated root runtime

The entry file's top-level runtime code becomes a dormant compiler-owned entry. Entry assembly activates it exactly once. This includes top-level bindings, function calls and other runtime statements. Helper files define reusable declarations and never own an entry or an export block.

### Page fragments

Two forms of direct top-level template in `@entry.moth` become page fragments:

- A const fragment uses the `#` prefix before the opening bracket. It folds at compile time and is emitted as static HTML.
- A runtime fragment uses the plain bracket form without `#`. The entry evaluates it when assembly activates the module, and publication waits until that invocation succeeds.

The builder preserves source order between static and runtime fragments. Static fragments remain in the document even when startup fails. Runtime fragments use generated slots, and the entry boundary fills those slots only after successful startup. Failure publishes none of that invocation's runtime fragments. This is not rewind: other side effects do not roll back.

The generated terminal handler presents a generic safe release notice, not the application's `Error` message as HTML. Development presentation, including the three startup categories and invocation-local reporting, is owned by `docs/build-system-design.md` > `Development entry-error reports` and summarised at @../../developer-docs/compiler-design/html-assembly-and-output/#document-shell-and-fragments (HTML assembly).

### Metadata-only and empty pages

An entry with only direct const builder slot contributions still produces a complete document. A page containing only resource-bearing metadata also emits the resources it uses without non-empty executable HIR, an artificial fragment or generated runtime code.

An empty `@entry.moth` produces the builder's ordinary default document for its route. The route exists because the entry file exists.

### Dependency entries

An imported module never activates its entry. When a module is bound for its public API, its entry runtime, entry body and page fragments remain dormant. Only exported declarations remain available.

A consumer cannot make a dependency's root work or fragments part of its own entry, and imported entries do not contribute named builder slots to the consumer. An entry-only child publishes no normal API, so a dependency that requests one receives a missing-surface diagnostic. Valid dependency access and `check` remain non-activating.

### Do not put page fragments in helper files

Helper files define reusable declarations. The entry file binds and uses them. Top-level page templates and builder slots belong in `@entry.moth` only. An ordinary file cannot become a page or contribute a fragment by containing a template.

### Concepts related to entry runtime

- For module roles and root filenames see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For builder slots and command policy see @#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
- For routes and single-file pages see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).
